meta {
  name: List Investigations
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{probity_base_url}}/investigations
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  ~limit: {{probity_limit}}
  ~type: alert
  ~status: investigating
  ~severity: high
  ~classification: fraud_risk
  ~category: relationship
  ~group_by: vendor_rule
  ~cursor: 
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{probity_api_key}}
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: false
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Alerts and vendor findings that have entered the investigation workflow,
  unioned and ordered by last activity (newest first). Requires
  `investigations:read`.
  
  Filters: type (alert | finding), status, severity.
  
  Response: { data: Investigation[], next_cursor, has_more }
  Investigation: { type, id, rule_code, title, classification, category,
                   severity, status, vendor_id, vendor_name, transaction_id,
                   detected_at, last_activity_at, due_date, note_count }
  
  Self-sufficient for a queue/ticket: rule_code is the stable id, title the
  display name, vendor_name the counterparty (customer keys only — omitted for
  partner keys), transaction_id the linked transaction (null for findings),
  detected_at when Probity detected it (vs last_activity_at lifecycle),
  note_count activity (>0 = someone has worked it).
  
  The cursor is composite — always pass next_cursor back unchanged; don't
  construct one.
  
  `group_by=vendor_rule` collapses items sharing (vendor_id, rule_code) into one
  rollup row: { rule_code, vendor_id, vendor_name, title, classification,
  category, highest_severity, item_count, alert_count, finding_count,
  first_detected_at, last_activity_at, items[{type,id}] }. This is how a vendor's
  repeated detections stop reading as duplicate queue rows. Omit for one-row-per-item.
}
